{"name":"Pewter Tankard","hex":"#a39b90","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pewter","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An alloy of approximately 93–98% tin and 1–2% copper, and the balance of antimony.","An alloy of tin and lead."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark, dull grey colour, like that of the metal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pewter"},{"term":"tankard","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large drinking vessel, sometimes of pewter, sometimes with a glass base, with one handle and often a hinged cover."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tankard"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pewter","blocks":[{"text":"Pewter is a malleable metal alloy consisting of tin (85–99%), antimony, copper (2%), bismuth, and sometimes silver. In the past, it was an alloy of tin and as much as 40% lead, but most modern pewter, in order to prevent lead poisoning, is not made with lead. Pewter has a low melting point, around 170–230 °C (338–446 °F), depending on the exact mixture of metals. The word pewter is possibly a variation of \"spelter\", a term for zinc alloys."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pewter","wikidata":"Q852372"},{"title":"Tankard","blocks":[{"text":"A tankard is a form of drinkware consisting of a large, roughly cylindrical, drinking cup with a single handle. In recent centuries tankards were typically made of silver or pewter, but they can be made of other materials, for example glass, wood, pottery, or boiled leather. A tankard may have a hinged lid, and tankards featuring glass bottoms are also fairly common. Beer steins have a similar shape and use."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/MaryRose-wooden_tankard1.JPG/330px-MaryRose-wooden_tankard1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tankard","wikidata":"Q3446398"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Fork-Viking-Pewter-detail.jpg/250px-Fork-Viking-Pewter-detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Fork-Viking-Pewter-detail.jpg/250px-Fork-Viking-Pewter-detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Detail on a pewter fork handle from Norway, showing three scenes: King Olaf II Haraldsson (St. Olaf), his men, and a Viking ship","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFork-Viking-Pewter-detail.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Pewter_Ring.jpg/250px-Pewter_Ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Pewter_Ring.jpg/330px-Pewter_Ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Pewter_Ring.jpg/250px-Pewter_Ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APewter_Ring.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Pewterplate_exb.jpg/250px-Pewterplate_exb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/91/Pewterplate_exb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Pewterplate_exb.jpg/250px-Pewterplate_exb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APewterplate_exb.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Pewter_vase.jpg/250px-Pewter_vase.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/th